sentences.t:
Fincadas son las tiendas e pareçen los alvores.
a una grand priessa tañién los atamores;
alegravas mio Çid e dixo: “tan buen día es oy!”

sentences.l:
Fixed are the tents and appear the dawn-lights.
at a great haste were-beating the drums;
was-gladdening-himself my Çid and said: “so good day is today!”

sentences.t:
Vi[d]olo el atalaya e tanxo el esquila;
prestas son las mesnadas de las yentes de [Roy Díaz],
adóbanse de coraçón e dan salto de la villa.

sentences.l:
Saw-it the watchman and rang the bell;
ready are the household-troops of the peoples of [Roy Díaz],
they-arm-themselves of heart and give leap from the town.

sentences.t:
Dos fallan con los moros cometiénlos tan aína,
sácanlos de las huertas mucho a fea guisa;
quinientos mataron dellos conplidos en es día.

sentences.l:
Where-they find-themselves with the Moors attack-them so quickly,
they-drive-them from the gardens very at ugly way;
five-hundred they-killed of-them complete on that day.

sentences.t:
Tornados son a mio Çid los que comién so pan;
él se lo vío con los ojos, cuéntangelo delant,
alegre es mio Çid por quanto fecho han:

sentences.l:
Returned are to my Çid those who were-eating his bread;
he himself it saw with the eyes, they-tell-it-to-him before,
glad is my Çid for how-much done they-have:

sentences.t:
“Oídme, cavalleros, non rastará por al;
oy es día bueno e mejor será cras:
por la mañana prieta todos armados seades,

sentences.l:
“Hear-me, knights, not it-will-remain for other;
today is day good and better will-be tomorrow:
by the morning dark all armed you-may-be,

sentences.t:
Fablava Minaya, non lo quiso detardar:
“pues esso queredes, Çid, a mí mandedes al;
dadme çiento e treinta cavalleros pora huebos de lidiar;

sentences.l:
Was-speaking Minaya, not it he-wanted to-delay:
“since that you-want, Çid, to me you-may-order other;
give-me hundred and thirty knights for need of fighting;

sentences.t:
quando vos los f[ór]edes ferir, entraré yo del otra part;
o de amas o del una Dios nos valdrá.”
Essora dixo el Çid: “de buena voluntad.”

sentences.l:
when you them may-g[o] to-strike, I-will-enter I from-the other side;
or from both or from-the one God us will-help.”
Then said the Çid: “of good will.”

sentences.t:
A los mediados gallos, antes de la mañana.
el obispo don Jero[me] la missa les cantava;
la missa dicha grant sultura les dava:

sentences.l:
At the middle cocks, before of the morning.
the bishop don Jero[me] the mass to-them was-singing;
the mass said great absolution to-them he-was-giving:

sentences.t:
A vos Çid don Rodrigo, en buen[a] çinxiestes espada,
yo vos canté la missa por aquesta mañana;
pídovos un[a] don[a] e seam presentad[a]:

sentences.l:
To you Çid don Rodrigo, in good[-one] you-girded sword,
I to-you sang the mass for this morning;
I-ask-of-you [a] gif[t] and may-it-be-to-me presente[d]:
